<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Calvary</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Golgotha</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Calvary, or Golgotha, was a site outside the walls of Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> where, according to the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">canonical Gospels</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> was crucified. The names mean &quot;place of the Skull&quot;, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> Calvariae and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> Golgolta. Tradition places it within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-holy-sepulchre">Church of the Holy Sepulchre</a>, a site traditionally believed to have been identified by the empress Helena, mother of Constantine, on her visit to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a> in 325. In the 19th century Protestant scholars proposed another location near the Garden Tomb, about 500 m to the north. It has been a pilgrimage destination since at least the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/calvary.jpg" alt="Calvary" width="330" height="229" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Berthold Werner · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jerusalem_Holy_Sepulchre_BW_18.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
